**Proposition 8.1 (effective PH37 realization and complete sufficient budget).** The composite transformation $A_0\mapsto A\mapsto M_A$ is computable into the published $\mathfrak D_\mu(B)$ of Convention 2.1. It admits a total computable finite sufficient budget $B_{A_0}$ including every installed description bit and every simultaneously resident/private microstate bit. It requires no real probability register, posterior row, history archive or length-dependent runtime storage.

**Proof.** Lemma 7.2 produces finite simple tables. Construction 3.2 computes the finite balanced codebook and its prefix automaton, then takes its product with a single $Q$ label, the suffix checker, validity flag and original finite $C_0$. The product is finite and explicitly enumerable from the input table and the once-fixed original $\mathcal T_0$ and dictionary. At a code completion, a deterministic row needs no random bit and a half/half row needs one. Early emission uses one fair bit. Probabilities $u,d,v$ use respectively 3, 4, 5 fresh fair bits: emit $\alpha$ exactly when all bits are zero. Every intermediate bit prefix, bit position and return PC is a finite sampler state. The same finite update routine handles synthetic and actual letters. Each letter performs finite original table lookup, private update and bounded sampler work. No retry count or unbounded arithmetic is required by this construction.

Here is a concrete sufficient serialization and workspace rule, so “finite” does not conceal an uncharged address or interpreter. First expand the finite returned-state product into a finite logical instruction graph $G$ with $N\ge1$ nodes. Its nodes include all parser and original control labels, per-letter sampler prefixes and PCs, update branches, finite event/render positions and provider/output-buffer flags. The finite original event blocks can be expanded into one output node per event. There is no stack or growing output archive. Node opcodes distinguish deterministic, fair-bit, external-Read, sole external-Stop, synthetic-letter/output, and return/exit cases. A fixed mode flag permits an external operation only on the actual track. Each node has at most three outgoing slots; pad unused slots with an unused fixed successor. At a fair node only two slots are used. At an actual input node the slots encode the two possible responses to one original Read and the sole legal Stop transition, with original legality flags. They are not separately requested letter actions or new menu choices.

Number nodes by $0,\ldots,N-1$, put $w=\max\{1,\lceil\log_2N\rceil\}$, and let $A_E$ count the finite event/output alphabet including silent output. Let $\sigma=\max\{1,\lceil\log_2A_E\rceil\}$. Every row stores a three-bit opcode, three legality bits, three $w$-bit successors and one $\sigma$-bit output label. Current-record rendering uses the complete installed original dictionary; its rendering symbols and position states are finite and included in $G$. Include the *entire* original table/dictionary length $T_0$ of [PH] Definition 25.1, even if the graph already redundantly encodes its transitions. Install self-delimiting metadata $H_A$ for $N,w,A_E,\sigma$, original table lengths, initial nodes and alphabet meanings. The graph begins after this self-delimiting header and the original table follows the fixed-width graph rows; their offsets are computed in charged slots after parsing, avoiding a self-referential header size. All installed header values are computed directly from the finite graph and original data. The static graph/data length is exactly, for this chosen format,

$$
T_A=H_A+N(6+3w+\sigma)+T_0.
\tag{8.1}
$$

Choose one fixed sequential-bit interpreter and loader for these rows, with code/constants length $I$. Its instructions are PH30.2 read/write-current-bit, one-cell movement, fixed Boolean branch, one fresh fair bit and single-symbol buffered output. It scans ROM sequentially using charged head/address fields, copies fields one bit at a time and branches by fixed opcodes. Moving to a selected graph row costs all intervening cell movements; successor indices are stored bit strings, never free pointers. Every integer operation below is a finite bit-loop with charged PC, carry and scratch. Thus the finite graph macrosteps compile to exactly PH37 microsteps with probabilities $0,1/2,1$. The emission bits and any random label-update bit are fresh disjoint calls, giving the joint kernel $q_z(x)P_x(z,z')$ after service elimination. Arithmetic and table lookup are finite bit routines, not oracles. Its implementation uses the following sixteen main integer slots: actual and synthetic graph node; actual and synthetic original-$C_0$ index; event position; rendering position; table address; row base; field offset; loop index; field length; two arithmetic temporaries; saved return PC; interpreter PC; output-symbol index. Allocate sixteen additional integer slots for input/image, code, ROM and work head positions, sequential-scan/bit-copy positions, instruction operands, symbol-bit position and temporary loop/field metadata. All of these are explicit finite arrays, not free interpreter control. This gives thirty-two integer slots in total. Fixed-label loops are inlined where necessary, so no dynamic stack or recursive call frame is used. Allocate 64 further Boolean slots for mode/idle/permission flags, carry/borrow, the current input/output/fair bit, provider request/response and finite output handshake flags. Five-bit sampling prefixes are already represented in graph nodes; allocating an additional slot for them within those reserved bits is harmless redundant charging. All oper