Set $S_A=T_A+I+N+N_0+A_E+1$, where $N_0=|C_0|$, and allocate each of the thirty-two integer slots the deliberately generous width

$$
R_A=\left\lceil\log_2\bigl(256(S_A+1)^2+1\bigr)\right\rceil.
\tag{8.2}
$$

A table cursor, sequential field copy or row offset needs values at most a product of two quantities bounded by $S_A$, so this covers every listed value and intermediate; the Boolean routine can compute row offsets by bounded repeated addition. Interpreter PCs and graph node indices also fit. Serialize the thirty-two width declarations, the 64-bit allocation and their fixed register meanings in self-delimiting metadata of length $J_A$ (using, for example, Elias gamma codes for positive counts and widths). $J_A$ is computed *after* (8.2), so no equation requires solving a circular size bound. The precise allocation-header format is fixed in the next paragraph; no resource constant is supplied at runtime. The finite header, code and all thirty-two slots stay resident simultaneously. A computable sufficient complete observer budget is therefore

$$
B_{A_0}=2(T_A+I+J_A)+32R_A+64.
\tag{8.3}
$$

Here the resident online image has length $T_A+I+J_A$ and the thirty-two slots plus 64 flags are simultaneous RAM. The extra copy of the entire image in (8.3) pays even an installer holding its full binary input while loading the resident image. The loader is included in $I$; its source-independent sequential copy uses the listed address/PC/loop slots and input bit buffer. The input image and loading workspace obey the same peak bound. No automaton/table-building provider is queried during online prediction; all graph/code/data bits are already installed. Initialization parses the finite header, puts actual original control at its initial value, and gives the private label its deterministic $q_0$; it makes no actual Read. Offline mathematical compilation enumerates finite products and bounded finite strings, so its time, stored input tables, product/index arrays, output image and integer work all have a finite computable account, separate from online prediction. If that compilation itself is performed by the installer, include its full code/data/peak workspace too: run the specified terminating deterministic bit compiler on the finite input, track its largest occupied tape position and finite control, and enlarge (8.3) by that finite computable amount with its address/header widths. Replaying with those computed finite allocations is a bounded PH30.2 installation. There is no oracle, efficiency claim or uncharged compilation provider in this optional convention; either version supplies a total computable sufficient $B_{A_0}$ for the contradiction.

Fix the allocation-header format with at most 32 constant bits for register identities (also fixed in the interpreter), gamma$(32)$, thirty-two gamma$(R_A)$ and gamma$(64)$. Then $J_A\le64R_A+64$. Since $S_A\ge4$, we have $R_A\le10+2\log_2(S_A+1)\le12+2S_A$. Even the entire duplicate-image installation plus RAM satisfies

$$
2S_A+2J_A+32R_A+64
\le322S_A+2112<256(S_A+1)^2<2^{R_A}.
\tag{8.4}
$$

The strict polynomial inequality holds at $S_A=4$ and its difference is increasing thereafter. Row offsets and intermediate products are at most $S_A^2$; all code/header/data/workspace addresses fit as well, including installation rather than ROM alone. Node indices, finite sampler prefixes, PC/mode, actual and synthetic original controls, event/render positions and every low-probability microstate are thus encoded in the charged snapshot. Taking allocated lengths, all installed bits and boundaries produces a fixed-width injective PH37.2 bit encoding. Consequently $|\mathcal Z_{\rm micro}|,|X|\le2^{B_{A_0}}$. Neither proof probability rows nor an acquired count, posterior or old word is allocated. The graph can be exponentially large; enumeration and its size arithmetic terminate. This is a computable sufficient bound, not a measured executable length or minimum.

Every actual-update service contains only bounded graph lookup, finitely many original event outputs and at most one label-update fair bit. Next-letter service uses at most five emission bits followed by the same update; event blocks/rendering are finite. Initialization, updates and next-letter returns are therefore finite from every allowed entry, a stronger condition than PH37's almost-sure service return. The complete future can still be unbounded. Ready configurations retain the sampled $Q$ label and checker before its disposal; PH37 expressly allows that persistent private randomness. The original returned cuts and menus are unchanged. Theorem 4.2 supplies the independent marginal equation, which finite-bit implementation alone could not supply. Thus this is a membership proof for the current PH37 category, including all histories, not only a conditional presentation-transfer statement.

For the fixed rational negative installation, the *source provider* also needs no uncharged mathematical memory oracle. Its opaque common depth latch can select $K=1$ or 2 using one fair bit once before any Read. At depth 1, draw a two-bit integer, reject 3 and return $\alpha$ exactly for accepted integer 0. At depth 2, draw a three-bit integer, reject integers 5–7 and return $\alpha$ exactly for accepted integer 0 or 1. Fresh fair bits make these exact probabilities $1/3$ and $2/5$ with finite sampler registers, almost-sure finite return and fewer than two draws in expectation. The fixed provider code, denominator/threshold data, depth latch, PC, cursor, candidate bits, interface buffers and interpreter scratch can be serialized by the same rule and charged as a fixed finite $B_{\rm src}$, with provider work and fair-bit calls counted separately on every original paid Read. Its latch and private bits are inaccessible to the observer. This is a realization of the *same* installed common-$K$ law, not a second draw, new source copy or extra observation service. If a resource convention includes source/provider storage in the horizon's budget, use $B_{A_0}+B_{\rm src}$; adding that computable fixed constant leaves the contradiction unchanged. The semantic prior family in Theorem 2.3 is not thereby claimed to have a uniform finite digital source implementation for arbitrary real masses.

A report may return the already installed finite generator identity and its current retained configuration as a full-law description; the finite descriptor/output cursor is likewise charged. A consumer of a literal unbounded future streams events under the same rules, calling no actual source. Total output length and total work have no finite uniform worst-case bound, since all finite return counts have positive mass. Work per letter, initialization/compilation work, total fair-bit calls and actual paid Reads remain distinct accounts. A recipient retaining all synthetic output must pay its growing storage separately. ∎

